home = f'''<section class="hero-review" id="proposal-update"><div class="wrap hero-review-grid"><div><h1>Two proposals for the Foundation.</h1><p class="hero-lead">One asks for 0xPhilipp’s removal and a controlled transition. The other sets community governance rules and independent oversight for every director.</p><p>Each proposal stands independently. The revised transition requires <strong>all three directors to approve</strong> until a fourth joins, then three approvals. Proposal 2 can operate with the existing Board if Proposal 1 fails.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="#two-proposals">Compare the proposals {ARROW}</a><a class="button secondary" href="{PROPOSALS_URL}">Read the latest texts</a></div><p class="hero-caveat">Proposal summary updated 16 November 2026. These are discussion texts; the proposed rules are not presented as enacted. Ballot evidence below remains the dated 14 November capture.</p></div><aside class="vote-deadline" aria-label="Final 4855 and A296 results"><p class="status-live">Final vote records</p><h2>4855 passed on 12 November.</h2><dl><div><dt>YES, excluding abstain</dt><dd>{number(final_yes4855, 2)}%</dd></div><div><dt>A296 YES weight</dt><dd>50 / 100</dd></div><div><dt>A296 threshold</dt><dd>75%</dd></div></dl><p>A296 expired on <strong>14 November at 01:43:56 UTC</strong> (13 November, 6:43:56 p.m. MST). Rose and Vini voted YES. Neither 0xPhilipp nor MB cast a ballot.</p><a href="{REFORM_RECORD}proposal-4855.json">4855 final result</a> · <a href="{A296_URL}">A296 resolution</a></aside></div></section>
<div class="wrap">
<section class="section" id="two-proposals"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Two separate decisions</h2><p>Support for governance reform does not require support for removing a particular director.</p></div><div class="two-col">
<article><h3>Proposal 1: removal and controlled transition</h3><p>Withdraw confidence in 0xPhilipp and pursue lawful removal, preserve the fifth-director election, and elect a replacement separately. Verify operational continuity and complete the specified handover with independent checks.</p><p><strong>All directors must approve until a fourth director is appointed, after which three approvals are required.</strong> That means 3-of-3, then 3-of-4 and 3-of-5. Recusal does not lower the minimum.</p><p><a href="{PROPOSAL_1}">Read Proposal 1 and its exact amendment {ARROW}</a></p></article>
<article><h3>Proposal 2: governance and independent accountability</h3><p>Require implementation of passed community decisions within lawful authority, commission an independent financial and contract-authority review, and apply conflict and payment controls to all directors.</p><p>Maintain a public decision register, regular accountability reports and a community-approved framework for Board versus community decisions. This proposal removes no named director and grants no two-approval permission.</p><p><a href="{PROPOSAL_2}">Read Proposal 2 and its exact amendments {ARROW}</a></p></article></div><p class="note">Neither proposal can use the other’s unadopted powers to authorize itself. Passage, legal adoption and contract execution are separate events. The links above preserve the 16 November revision; <a href="{PROPOSALS_URL}">the latest discussion texts</a> may change.</p></section>
<section class="section reading" id="proposal-reasoning"><h2>Why we propose these changes</h2>
<p>4855 approved a public election for a fifth director and community elections for future appointments. A296 was the Board resolution to implement it. Its two YES votes supplied 50/100 weight against a 75% threshold, so it expired and is recorded as rejected. There were no NO or ABSTAIN ballots. Either remaining director could have supplied the third YES; the record alone does not establish why either did not vote. At the 14 November evidence capture, 4855’s seven-day deadline to publish implementation owners had not expired. This historical capture does not establish a later missed deadline.</p>
<p>Our case for Proposal 2 is that failed ratification shows the need for a clear duty to implement passed Terra governance decisions. The proposed amendments would remove a second discretionary policy veto while preserving lawful-authority limits, non-waivable duties and case-specific execution judgment. A specific legal or technical obstacle would require disclosure, independent assessment and a lawful remedy, with unaffected work continuing.</p>
<h3>Why remove 0xPhilipp?</h3>
<p>The proposal argues for his removal based on the wider record: his stated position that ordinary community appointment votes are advisory, his support for A272’s removal and threshold change, beneficiary participation in payment approvals, and unresolved contract-control and milestone-acceptance questions. Those issues are documented below and in the full proposal. A296 adds an implementation failure involving two nonvoting directors; it does not establish that 0xPhilipp alone caused the failure or acted with a particular motive.</p>
<p>0xPhilipp argues that amendments and appointments require the Foundation’s governing procedures, eligibility checks and legal review. The proposed amendments retain those steps while removing a discretionary second veto over the community’s decision.</p>
<p>A272 failed while other Board business continued. The rejection of 4853 did not settle allegations against any director. The full proposal includes 0xPhilipp’s responses, A291’s proposed safeguards and his technical contributions. The payment and governance rules would apply to every director. This proposal seeks only 0xPhilipp’s removal.</p>
<h3>How the transition is constrained</h3>
<p>The 16 November revision removes the earlier temporary 2-of-3 permission. All three remaining directors must approve until a fourth is appointed. If the fifth-director election finishes before removal and four directors remain, three approvals are required. Proposal 1 preserves the ongoing election and does not delay it to create a three-director interval.</p>
<p>Discretionary interim authority ends after 60 calendar days from activation, or earlier when the fourth director joins. After day 60, limited caretaker acts still require all three directors. New related-party funding, increased director compensation and new long-term commitments are prohibited during the three-director interim configuration, subject only to separately authorized exceptions. If conflicts prevent approval, a lawful contingency or specific further authorization is needed; recusal cannot reduce the minimum.</p>
<h3>What follows passage</h3>
<p>Each proposal directs PD to acknowledge passage and name responsible implementers within <strong>seven calendar days</strong>, and publish an implementation dossier within <strong>21 calendar days of passage</strong>. Both clocks start at passage. The dossier must identify governing documents, lawful adoption and enforcement routes, independent appointments, funding, contract control and dated completion plans. Missing items require a specific gap, responsible person, next action and completion date. Progress is reported weekly.</p>
<p>These are post-vote implementation duties. The texts do not require all supporting materials before submission, authorize unspecified expenditure or allow later documents to change the approved powers and safeguards. Additional funding and material changes need the applicable further approvals.</p>
<h3>Handover must be demonstrated</h3>
<p>Both proposals preserve the original TLA commitments to transfer administration after one month and ownership permissions after three months. They credit completed transfers and require a current contract-by-contract schedule, including TLA global configuration and Foundation-used Treasury and v3 contracts, with direct and indirect control paths.</p>
<p>Source, build, licence and maintenance-access records, independent operating-readiness checks and a costed contingency plan belong in the 21-day dossier. A transfer of one role does not prove all upstream control transferred; a missing public repository does not establish what access or rights exist. The proposals do not confer ownership of unrelated ERIS infrastructure or authorize an automatic withdrawal or shutdown.</p>
<p><a href="/governance/#proposed-amendments">Review the accountability deadlines and adoption safeguards</a>. Removal is a confidence decision; it does not establish wrongdoing. Independent review must consider all relevant directors, preserve responses and distinguish missing evidence from established failures.</p>
{source_list([(PROPOSAL_1, 'Proposal 1: removal and transition — 16 November text'), (PROPOSAL_2, 'Proposal 2: governance and accountability — 16 November text'), (REFORM_FORUM, 'Background discussion on the Phoenix forum'), (REFORM_RECORD + 'proposal-4855.json', '4855: final chain record'), (REFORM_RECORD + 'a296-proposal.json', 'A296: rejected resolution and threshold'), (REFORM_RECORD + 'a296-votes.json', 'A296: complete ballot response'), ('https://forum.phoenix.money/t/phoenix-foundation-community-selection-of-a-fifth-director-and-future-appointments/70/4', '0xPhilipp’s response to the appointment proposal'), (REFORM_RECORD + 'manifest.json', 'Source URLs, capture times and hashes')])}</section>
<section class="section section-grid" id="what-4855-does"><div class="section-intro"><h2>4855, in plain language</h2><p>Four directors can split two against two. A fifth gives the board another equal voice to reach a decision.</p></div><div>
<p><strong>The community would choose the new director.</strong> Volunteers would have 14 days to put themselves forward, followed by seven days for public questions. Each candidate would then face a separate Terra governance vote. Voters could support more than one candidate.</p>
<p><strong>Three directors would have to agree.</strong> The intended result is five equal seats and at least three affirmative approvals for multisig decisions. The fifth director would have one vote, the same as everyone else. Existing directors would remain in office.</p>
<p>The Foundation’s Treasury would fund the candidate-ballot deposits. The selected candidate would have to complete identity and eligibility checks before joining. The proposal also asks for a bylaw change so future directors are chosen through community elections.</p>
<p><strong>Passage approved this process; it did not elect a candidate.</strong> The Foundation still has to complete the appointment, change the multisig and adopt the bylaw amendment through its governing procedures. The chain vote does not execute those changes automatically.</p>
{source_list([('https://www.mintscan.io/terra/proposals/4855', 'Passed proposal 4855'), ('https://gist.github.com/PFC-developer/e3ac3425ef97b0e4df3d1191d9e8fdc8/7a0554a86ae595bda227e2731e46a6949983e726', 'Full submitted proposal and election rules')])}</div></section>
<section class="section" id="why-ampluna-matters"><span id="ampluna"></span><div class="section-heading"><h2>Why the ampLUNA voting bloc matters</h2><p>A small group of participants can decide how a much larger pool of LUNA votes on who oversees the Foundation.</p></div>
<p class="context-note">The ampLUNA analysis below preserves the {AMP_CAPTURE_LABEL} snapshot and separately dated lock queries. Its voting figures and contract permissions describe those captures. 4855 has since passed. At the voting snapshot, YES was {number(yes4855, 2)}% excluding abstentions, VETO was {number(veto4855, 2)}% of all votes, and participation was {number(quorum4855, 2)}%. <a href="{AMP_RECORD}tally-4855.json">Historical tally</a>.</p>
<p>ampLUNA uses an internal vote before casting the hub’s Terra ballot. Users who lock ampLUNA receive internal voting power, called vAMP. Once participants supply at least 10% of that power, their voting split directs the hub’s whole ballot.</p>
<p>For 4855, two addresses supplied <strong>{number(amp_participation, 2)}% of the internal voting power</strong>. Both chose NO WITH VETO, so the hub cast its entire ballot that way. The other internal voting power does not automatically count as abstaining. This does not show that all ampLUNA holders chose or supported that position.</p>
<p>One voter is the publicly linked wallet of <strong>0xPhilipp, a current PF director</strong>; the other is the ERIS protocol-fee recipient. The concern is the extent to which this small participation can shape a vote about the Foundation’s own governance. A director has a direct interest in that subject, and the two addresses do not establish two independent decision-makers.</p>
<p>The hub’s direct ballot overrides its validators’ votes for the hub’s delegations. That gives these internal votes influence beyond their own locked holdings. These facts warrant scrutiny of representation and independence; they do not establish improper conduct or determine the final result of 4855.</p>
{source_list([(AMP_RECORD + 'gauge-voters-4855.json', 'The two internal votes'), (AMP_RECORD + 'hub-vote-4855.json', 'The hub’s NO WITH VETO ballot'), ('#ampluna-voters', 'Wallet connections and evidence limits')])}</section>
<section class="section" id="ampluna-locks"><div class="section-heading"><h2>How much did the two wallets lock?</h2><p>Two wallets locked the equivalent of about <strong>{number(amp_lock_luna)} LUNA</strong>. Their votes set the ballot on proposal 4855 for the hub, which had about <strong>{number(amp_stake / 1000000, 2)} million LUNA</strong> delegated at the snapshot.</p></div>
<p>The ERIS fee wallet locked <strong>{number(amp_fee_principal, 6)} ampLUNA</strong>, worth about <strong>{number(amp_fee_principal * amp_rate, 2)} LUNA</strong> at the captured rate. Its lock expires on <strong>{amp_unlock_date(amp_fee_lock)}</strong>. The ERIS validator wallet locked <strong>{number(amp_validator_principal, 6)} ampLUNA</strong>, worth about <strong>{number(amp_validator_principal * amp_rate, 2)} LUNA</strong>. Its lock expires on <strong>{amp_unlock_date(amp_validator_lock)}</strong>. Both unlock dates start at 00:00 UTC.</p>
<p>On 3 November, two days before voting opened, the fee wallet extended its lock by 47 weeks and deposited no additional ampLUNA in that transaction. Holding the other votes and locks fixed, the two wallets would have supplied <strong>{number(amp_no_extension, 2)}%</strong> of eligible voting power without the extension. With it they supplied <strong>{number(amp_participation, 2)}%</strong>, clearing the <strong>10% quorum</strong>.</p>
<p>The voting keys directed the hub ballot on 4855. They gave the voters no permission to withdraw or spend the pool’s LUNA. The fee wallet’s escrow history starts at 1,000 ampLUNA in February 2023 and records later increases. The source of the deposited tokens remains unverified.</p>
<p class="note">The LUNA equivalents use the hub exchange rate captured on {amp_rate_date}. The pool size comes from the earlier ballot snapshot above.</p>
{source_list([(AMP_RECORD + 'lock-evidence.json', 'Both lock positions, the exchange rate and the extension transaction'), (AMP_RECORD + 'lock-evidence.json.source.md', 'Capture method and limits'), (AMP_RECORD + 'hub-state.json', 'Pool size at the ballot snapshot')])}</section>
<section class="section" id="how-ampluna-votes"><div class="section-heading"><h2>How escrow votes become a chain vote</h2><p>Holding ampLUNA alone does not create a separate Terra ballot for the LUNA backing it. The staking hub holds those delegations.</p></div>
<ol class="amp-vote-flow"><li><strong>Lock and vote</strong><p>Users lock ampLUNA in the voting escrow. Their voting power includes a fixed component and a time-dependent component, calculated for the proposal’s end period. They choose YES, NO, ABSTAIN or NO WITH VETO.</p></li><li><strong>Reach the 10% quorum</strong><p>The proposal gauge is currently configured for a 10% quorum of escrow voting power. At this snapshot, two addresses supplied <b>{number(amp_participation, 2)}%</b>, both voting NO WITH VETO.</p></li><li><strong>Apply the participating split</strong><p>The gauge passes the participants’ weighted split to the hub. All recorded participation was NO WITH VETO, so the hub cast <b>100% NO WITH VETO</b> for its delegated stake.</p></li></ol>
<p>The quorum is a percentage of <strong>escrow voting power</strong>, not all ampLUNA holders or all LUNA delegated by the hub. Nonparticipants do not automatically receive an ABSTAIN share. For example, a qualifying 60/40 YES/NO split would instruct a 60/40 hub vote, subject to rounding.</p>
<p>A direct hub ballot overrides the underlying validators’ votes for the hub’s delegations. Other delegators retain their own voting rights. The reported {number(amp_stake / 1000000, 2)}M LUNA is a contemporaneous delegation measure, not a fixed final vote weight.</p>
{source_list([(AMP_RECORD + 'gauge-config.json', 'Current 10% quorum and weighted-vote setting'), (AMP_RECORD + 'gauge-4855.json', '4855 escrow voting totals'), (AMP_RECORD + 'hub-vote-4855.json', 'Hub’s native 4855 ballot'), (AMP_GAUGE_SOURCE + 'contracts/amp-governance/prop_gauges/src/vote.rs', 'Release source: calculation and forwarding'), (AMP_GAUGE_SOURCE + 'packages/eris/src/prop_gauges.rs', 'Release source: quorum and weighted split')])}</section>
<section class="section" id="ampluna-voters"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Who supplied the two votes?</h2><p>The recorded addresses have different evidenced roles. Two addresses do not establish two independent people.</p></div>
{table(['Recorded voter', 'Share of escrow voting power', 'Vote', 'Established connection and limit'], amp_voter_rows, '4855 escrow voters at ' + AMP_CAPTURE_LABEL)}
<p>The fee-recipient address is a regular wallet with a single secp256k1 public key, not a smart contract. It is an ERIS operational account. ERIS projects have received PF grants, including Creda’s 210,000-USDC grant, but that relationship does not establish that PF funded this wallet’s voting position. The second address has a direct PF connection through 0xPhilipp’s stewardship and the recorded A266 grant. These links are relevant to independence; they do not establish improper voting or coordinated intent.</p>
<p>The fee-recipient wallet voted at <strong>13:30:35 UTC</strong> on 5 November; the validator wallet voted at <strong>13:33:01 UTC</strong>, <strong>2 minutes 26 seconds</strong> later. Both transactions used the memo <code>www.erisprotocol.com</code> and direct signing, with different public keys and no separate fee payer or granter. This is consistent with the same ERIS interface; it does not identify a shared person or machine. Blockchain records do not expose IP addresses or device identifiers.</p>
{source_list([(AMP_RECORD + 'fee-vote-tx.json', 'Fee-recipient vote transaction'), (AMP_RECORD + 'validator-gauge-vote-tx.json', 'Validator wallet’s gauge-vote transaction'), (AMP_RECORD + 'fee-recipient-account.json', 'Fee-recipient account type and public key')])}
{source_list([(AMP_RECORD + 'gauge-voters-4855.json', 'Both recorded voters and their exact voting power'), (AMP_RECORD + 'hub-config.json', 'Fee-recipient address in the hub configuration'), (AMP_RECORD + 'proposal-4853.json', '4853 proposer address and final status'), ('https://daodao.zone/dao/terra1k8ug6dkzntczfzn76wsh24tdjmx944yj6mk063wum7n20cwd7lxq4lppjg/proposals/A266', 'A266 grant proposal'), ('/stakeholders/#creda-approval', 'Creda approvals, funding and qualifications')])}</section>
<section class="section" id="ampluna-history"><div class="section-heading"><h2>How many wallets voted before?</h2><p>The five latest completed proposals still recorded in the ampLUNA voting gauge. These are not the five latest Terra proposals.</p></div>
{table(['Proposal', 'Voting ended (UTC)', 'Unique wallets', 'Recorded total (vAMP)', 'Share of eligible vAMP'], amp_history_rows, 'Completed gauge entries · records queried 5 November 2026, 15:48 UTC')}
<p><strong>Five distinct addresses appear across these five entries.</strong> The two addresses voting on 4855 are also the only recorded voters on 4853 and 4823. On 4853 both chose ABSTAIN; on 4823 both chose YES.</p>
<p>vAMP measures internal voting power, not LUNA or a count of tokens deposited. Totals above add YES, NO, ABSTAIN and NO WITH VETO. They are the retained totals for completed proposals; this check did not reconstruct contract state at each closing block. The owner can delete proposal records, so absence is not proof that a proposal was never offered.</p>
<p>At the 5 November query, the five latest completed <em>Terra</em> proposals were 4853, 4852, 4851, 4849 and 4848. Only 4853 has a retained gauge record in this query. The other four are reported as missing, not as proven zero turnout. The current 10% quorum is not assumed to have been the setting for every historical proposal.</p>
{source_list([('/record/ampluna-history/participation.json', 'Captured totals, all recorded voters and primary query URLs')])}</section>
<section class="section section-grid" id="ampluna-control"><div class="section-intro"><h2>Who can change the voting rules?</h2><p>The hub’s internal owner and its migration administrator hold different powers.</p></div><div>
<h3>ERIS configuration owner</h3><p>The live hub and proposal gauge name <code>terra1kefa…klgzl</code> as owner. This is a normal single-key chain account, not the PF multisig. That describes on-chain authorization, not how many people can access its key.</p><p>The hub owner can replace the authorized voting operator. Under the current code, the replacement operator can cast a normal or weighted hub vote without passing the escrow gauge’s quorum. The owner cannot directly vote while a different operator remains configured, but can change that configuration first.</p>
<p>The proposal-gauge owner can register proposals, choose their stored end time, change the quorum, switch between weighted and single-option voting, remove a user’s recorded votes from active proposals, and delete proposal records. Removing a vote record does not permanently bar that user from voting again.</p>
<h3>Phoenix Foundation migration administrator</h3><p>The hub’s migration admin is the PF DAO. Through its own approval process, it can authorize replacement hub code. The hub is now code 2726, version 1.8.0. Migration authority is broader than a permission to change today’s settings; replacement code could change the rules themselves. It does not make PF the current internal owner.</p>
<p>The voting gauge and escrow retain a separate migration admin, <code>terra1q0vny…q5tew</code>. The escrow also permits its owner or guardian to blacklist addresses and remove their voting power. These permissions are capabilities, not evidence that they were used to produce the captured 4855 vote.</p>
{details('Other hub powers and verification limits', '<p>The hub owner can change validator eligibility and delegation strategy, tune and rebalance delegations, change the fee recipient and reward fee within the current 10% cap (currently 8%), and adjust batch and expected-unbonding periods. The live periods are three days and 21 days. The v1.8.0 source rejects zero timing values but provides no operational upper bound beyond the integer type; changing these settings does not change Terra’s staking-module unbonding period.</p><p>The live hub, gauge and escrow bytecode hashes match their published release binaries. The associated release source was inspected; an independent reproducible build and complete security audit were not performed. In weighted mode, falling below quorum emits no replacement ballot, so an earlier chain ballot can remain. Deleting a gauge record does not itself retract that ballot.</p>')}
{source_list([(AMP_RECORD + 'hub-info.json', 'Live hub migration administrator and code ID'), (AMP_RECORD + 'gauge-info.json', 'Live gauge migration administrator'), (AMP_RECORD + 'release-verification.json', 'Deployed code and release-binary hash comparisons'), (AMP_HUB_SOURCE + 'contracts/hub/src/execute.rs', 'Hub owner permissions in release source'), (AMP_GAUGE_SOURCE + 'contracts/amp-governance/prop_gauges/src/contract.rs', 'Gauge owner permissions in release source'), (AMP_GAUGE_SOURCE + 'contracts/amp-governance/voting_escrow/src/contract.rs', 'Escrow blacklist authority in release source')])}</div></section>
<section class="section" id="4853-and-4855"><div class="section-heading"><h2>4853 and 4855 both ask governance to change PF’s governance</h2><p>The shared mechanism is a Terra governance mandate followed by implementation through the Foundation’s governing procedures. The proposed changes differ.</p></div>
{table(['Proposal', 'Requested change', 'What passage would execute'], [['4853 · rejected', 'An emergency supervisor to investigate the A272 dispute, with requested removal-facilitation powers. The published bylaws contain limits and tensions discussed in the historical review.', 'No executable messages. It did not automatically remove a director or change the multisig threshold.'], ['4855 · passed 12 November', 'Public nomination and election of a fifth equal director, at least three affirmative multisig approvals out of five, and specific amendments requiring community elections for future appointments.', 'No executable messages. Appointment requires eligibility/KYC and an authorized multisig change. Bylaw amendments require adoption under §7.3.']])}
<p>4853’s submission shows that a chain vote was already being used to seek changes to PF’s governance. Its rejection does not establish that its allegations were true or create an adopted legal precedent. 4855 uses an ordinary appointment route and does not declare an emergency or seek removal powers.</p>
<p>The governance issue is concrete: the community voted on who should participate in PF decisions, while the ampLUNA mechanism lets a qualifying group of escrow voters direct pooled LUNA in that same vote. Readers should be able to see the participating interests, the quorum and the owner powers when assessing how representative that vote is.</p>
{source_list([('https://www.mintscan.io/terra/proposals/4855', 'Proposal 4855 and latest voting status'), ('https://gist.github.com/PFC-developer/e3ac3425ef97b0e4df3d1191d9e8fdc8/7a0554a86ae595bda227e2731e46a6949983e726', 'Full 4855 text, pinned to the submitted revision'), ('/voting/#if-passes', 'Historical 4853 proposal, powers and limits'), ('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/phoenix-directive/docs/main/Phoenix%20Foundation%20-%20Bylaws.pdf#page=27', 'Bylaw amendment procedure: §7.3')])}
<p class="note">The snapshot uses LCD queries made in sequence and in parallel, bracketed by block queries; it is not a single-height tally. The <a href="{AMP_RECORD}manifest.json">capture manifest</a> records source URLs, timestamps and hashes. Voting was open at that capture; 4855 has since passed. The historical snapshot is retained for comparison.</p></section>
</div>
<div class="wrap"><section class="section" id="findings"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Four concerns in the records</h2></div><div class="finding-list"><article class="finding"><h3>0xPhilipp helped approve releases from ERIS’s Creda grant</h3><p>0xPhilipp proposed and voted YES on A209, enabling <strong>200,000 USDC</strong> for Creda’s first three milestones. Vini and MB supplied the other two YES votes. A215 later enabled another <strong>10,000 USDC</strong> for analytics, bringing the grant total to <strong>210,000 USDC</strong>.</p><p class="note">Both were three-director approvals. The Treasury action marks all four milestones claimed. These amounts funded the project; they do not establish personal profit or independent acceptance of the work.</p><a href="/stakeholders/#creda-approval">Creda’s grant, approval votes and milestones</a></article>
<article class="finding"><h3>The handover left some contract powers unchanged</h3><p>ERIS proposal 9 gave the Phoenix board upgrade control over two contracts. The hub’s internal owner and the ICS20 contract’s internal governance setting did not change. Other Treasury and TLA permissions also remain.</p><p class="note">A contract’s upgrade administrator and its internal owner can have different powers. The records identify authorized addresses; they do not establish who holds every key.</p><a href="/governance/#handover">Which powers transferred and which remain</a></article>
<article class="finding"><h3>Payment records do not show acceptance of every milestone</h3><p>Phoenix grants identified for Creda, Votion and the migration app total 265,000 USDC. Transfers and claimed milestones are recorded; independent acceptance of every deliverable has not been established.</p><p class="note">AADAO separately reports Votion’s Hydro work complete and jointly verified. Project funding is not personal profit, and a later unfinished page does not prove that no work occurred.</p><a href="/treasury/#project-funding">Funding, delivered work and missing acceptance records</a></article>
<article class="finding"><h3>A272 proposed concentrating board approval power</h3><p>A272 proposed removing Vini and lowering the approval threshold to 60%, allowing two of the remaining three seats to approve an action. It failed: 0xPhilipp and MB voted YES, Rose voted NO and Vini was absent.</p><p class="note">The board remained four seats with a 75% threshold. 4853 later sought a supervisor and removal-facilitation powers over the same dispute, but was rejected. A later removal and threshold change could reopen the concentration risk; neither happens automatically on passage.</p><a href="/voting/#removal-risk">The route from failed A272 to proposal 4853</a></article></div></section>
<section class="section section-grid" id="voting-power"><div class="section-intro"><h2>Historical 4853 voting scenario</h2></div><div><p>In the {CAPTURE_LABEL} snapshot, switching the ampLUNA hub to a full YES vote would raise YES from <strong>{number(base_metrics['yes_share_non_abstain_percent'], 2)}%</strong> to <strong>{number(scenario_metrics['yes_share_non_abstain_percent'], 2)}%</strong> of non-abstain votes, still below the required majority if nothing else changed.</p><p>The hub has a native ABSTAIN ballot, and its vAMP gauge records abstention voting power. The calculation models a change to that ballot; it does not establish a voting agreement.</p><a href="/voting/#scenario">See the assumptions and validator breakdown {ARROW}</a></div></section>
<section class="section" id="immediate"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Questions for continuing oversight</h2></div><ol class="action-list"><li><div><h3>Disclose financial interests</h3><p>Directors, Think Tank members, validators and contractors should disclose relevant payments, grants, hosting arrangements and voting interests under their public role or handle.</p></div><a href="/stakeholders/">Who has which role</a></li><li><div><h3>Publish voting instructions</h3><p>Explain planned hub and gauge votes. Where a validator follows a DAO mandate, finish the internal vote first. Lion DAO’s validator changed to YES while its A33 vote was still open.</p></div><a href="/voting/#lion-dao">Lion DAO’s voting sequence</a></li><li><div><h3>Provide the missing approval records</h3><p>Publish recusal decisions, milestone acceptance, contract handover terms and any approved extensions. Retain the ballots, payment records and responses for independent review.</p></div><a href="/governance/#reforms">Proposed governance changes</a></li></ol></section>
<section class="section reading"><h2>Check the sources</h2><p>The source library includes ballot exports, transaction responses and calculation files. The 27 October supplement checked 48 successful transactions, including 38 Terra transaction-byte hashes. Six cited transactions remain unavailable. This is not an independent audit of every transaction.</p><a href="/evidence/">Sources, verification method and limits {ARROW}</a></section></div>'''
home = home.replace('<section class="section reading"><h2>Check the sources</h2>', '<section class="section" id="contract-control"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Who controls the community’s assets?</h2><p>The control review focuses on TLA and Treasury: infrastructure funded to manage chain liquidity and community funds. TLA’s promised handover remains incomplete, and Treasury retains an ERIS upgrade administrator. Creda, Votion and Estima are treated separately as application-development grants.</p></div><p><a href="/control/">Read the TLA/Treasury terms, delivery and remaining risks</a></p><p class="note">Checked 16 November 2026. Retained powers create exposure; they do not establish misuse, private key custody or ERIS ownership of community assets.</p></section><section class="section reading"><h2>Check the sources</h2>', 1)
page('Two proposals for Foundation accountability', home, '/', 'Two independent proposals: removal and controlled transition; community governance and independent accountability. Three approvals remain required during the proposed transition.')

# Voting analysis.
voting = head('4853: rejected', '4853 was rejected on 31 October 2026. The analysis below preserves dated voting snapshots and explains the powers it proposed; these are not current voting figures. See the homepage for 4855.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><section class="reading"><h2>What 4853 proposes</h2><p>Proposal 4853 asks Terra governance to appoint the named emergency supervisor, Martin, to investigate the dispute surrounding A272. It also expressly asks for the removal-facilitation powers in bylaw Article 2.4.5.4. Those powers, and the limits on them, matter as much as the investigation.</p>{source_list([('https://ping.pub/terra/gov/4853', 'Proposal 4853 on Ping Pub'), (proposal['metadata'], 'Full proposal discussion on the Phoenix forum'), ('/record/audit/native_4853/proposal.json', 'Download the captured proposal response')])}</section>
<section id="if-passes"><div class="section-heading"><h2>What happens if 4853 passes?</h2></div>{table(['Step', 'Effect'], [['Appointment', 'The nominee must accept in writing and declare independence. The bylaws require acceptance within 30 days; otherwise the appointment is deemed rejected.'], ['Investigation', 'The supervisor is instructed to hear all four directors, review relevant records and decide remedies for the A272 dispute.'], ['Possible removal', 'Item 4 expressly invokes Article 2.4.5.4: instructions to facilitate a member’s removal where considered necessary or desirable to resolve the adverse event. It names no director for automatic removal.'], ['Duration and cost', 'Up to four weeks from written acceptance; an extension needs explicit Terra governance approval. Compensation is capped at USD 10,000.'], ['On-chain changes', 'The proposal contains no executable messages. It does not itself remove a member, change the 75% threshold, transfer contract roles or cancel grants. Any such change needs a further implementation step.']])}<p>The bylaws contain an important tension. Article 2.4.5.4 provides a route for removal instructions, while Article 2.6.2 limits the supervisor to investigation and remedies and restricts direct removal, replacement and direction of board decisions. The proposal invokes the former and remains subject to the latter. A written explanation of the permitted process is needed before treating either unrestricted removal power or a purely advisory role as settled.</p><p>Article 1.10 also allows the binding effect of the veto body to be disregarded during an emergency appointment. That is a weakening of a governance safeguard under the bylaws; it does not automatically disable a veto contract. Terra governance can remove the emergency supervisor under Article 2.6.4.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/native_4853/proposal.json', '4853: full text and empty execution-message list'), ('/record/evidence/exhibits/current/bylaws_current.pdf', 'Bylaws: Articles 1.10, 2.4.5.4, 2.6 and 3.1.3.2'), ('/record/audit/governance_4853/effects_review_20261027.json', 'Source-by-source effects review and conditional calculations')])}</section>
<section id="removal-risk"><div class="section-heading"><h2>A272 failed. Could 4853 achieve the same removal?</h2></div><p><strong>It could reopen a route to that outcome.</strong> On 3 October, 0xPhilipp proposed A272 to remove Vini and convert the board to two-of-three approval. It failed: 0xPhilipp and MB voted YES, Rose voted NO and Vini cast no ballot. On 24 October, the same proposer submitted 4853, defining the emergency around the unresolved A272 dispute and expressly seeking removal-facilitation powers.</p><p>The public discussion makes the concern concrete. In post 57, 0xPhilipp argued for separating removal from replacement and discussed a temporary two-of-three board. The final 4853 text does not guarantee that replacements join before a removal, maintain a minimum board size, or prevent two remaining directors from becoming sufficient approval authority.</p><p>This is an alternative process following a failed removal proposal. It is reasonable to scrutinize whether it could produce the same membership change without preserving the previous checks. The evidence does not establish that the supervisor will remove Vini or that the outcome has been agreed in advance.</p><h3>The proposer’s response</h3><p>4853 requires an independent, impartial review, hearing all four directors and examining any director’s conduct where relevant. In post 55, 0xPhilipp denied a voting agreement with MB and said a five-member, three-of-five board could be recommended. In post 57, 0xPhilipp offered an off-chain commitment to vote only NO or ABSTAIN during a temporary two-of-three period, except when onboarding new independent directors.</p><p class="note">That offer is material, but it is not an on-chain restriction or a condition written into 4853. A signed agreement and its enforcement have not been verified. In post 97, Vini withdrew support for the named candidate, citing an allegedly undisclosed ERIS relationship. The withdrawal is a public first-party statement; the alleged relationship has not been independently verified.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/phoenix/key_proposals.json', 'A272: removal message, threshold change and ballots'), (proposal['metadata'] + '/55', '0xPhilipp: possible five-member board and response to coordination claim'), (proposal['metadata'] + '/57', '0xPhilipp: removal before replacement and offered voting commitment'), (proposal['metadata'] + '/97', 'Vini: withdrawal of support for the candidate')])}</section>
<section id="remaining-board"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Would removing Vini leave grant interests in control?</h2></div><p>Removing Vini would remove a director who opposed the earlier Estima grant. It would leave 0xPhilipp’s ERIS/Creda grant relationship and MB’s Superbolt representative relationship in place. Removal alone would not resolve those interests, require their recusal from future payments, or complete the contract handover.</p><p>Vini was not the only recorded dissenting director. Rose also rejected Estima A258 and cast the only NO on A272. Vini has a separate 16,900-USDC audit-payment interest. Independent review should apply the same disclosure, recusal and acceptance standards to all four directors.</p>{table(['Possible configuration', 'YES votes required', 'Consequence'], [['Current four equal seats, 75%', '3 of 4', '0xPhilipp and MB need Rose or Vini.'], ['Vini removed, three equal seats, 75% unchanged', '3 of 3', '0xPhilipp and MB still need Rose. Removal alone does not give them two-person approval power.'], ['Three equal seats, threshold reduced to 60%', '2 of 3', 'Any two can approve without the third. This was A272’s proposed configuration; it is not automatically enacted by 4853.']])}<p class="note">These are conditional calculations for the Stewardship multisig, assuming equal seat weights. Board meetings and contract execution have different rules; Article 3.1.3.2(c) retains the signing threshold for actions requiring the multisig. Any later membership or threshold change must be assessed from its actual executable messages.</p><h3>Safeguards holders should ask for</h3><ul><li>Publish findings and let each affected director respond before a removal is implemented.</li><li>Review all four directors’ relevant grants, payment approvals and retained powers under the same written scope.</li><li>Require an approved replacement and threshold arrangement that prevents two interested directors from becoming sufficient approval authority.</li><li>Publish the supervisor’s independence declaration, any voting commitment, the treatment of the veto body and the exact removal or upgrade process.</li></ul>{source_list([('/stakeholders/#creda-approval', 'Creda’s 210,000-USDC grant and milestone approvals'), ('/stakeholders/#director-review', 'Vini and MB: payments, project relationships and dissent'), ('/record/audit/phoenix/director_profiles.json', 'Recorded director votes and their coverage'), ('/record/evidence/exhibits/current/bylaws_current.pdf', 'Bylaws and signing-threshold requirement')])}</section>
<section id="tally"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Tally at {CAPTURE_LABEL}</h2><p>{refresh['ballot_count']} ballots; {CAPTURE_LABEL}. YES was below the approval threshold; VETO was below 33.4%.</p></div>{table(['Option', 'LUNA', 'Reading'], [['YES', luna_count(tally['yes_count']), number(base_metrics['yes_share_non_abstain_percent'], 2) + '% of non-abstain votes'], ['NO', luna_count(tally['no_count']), '—'], ['NO WITH VETO', luna_count(tally['no_with_veto_count']), number(base_metrics['veto_share_all_percent'], 2) + '% of all votes; below 33.4%'], ['ABSTAIN', luna_count(tally['abstain_count']), 'Excluded from YES decision share']])}<p>Approval requires more than 50% YES among non-abstain votes, with VETO below 33.4% and participation meeting the 30% quorum. Captured participation is approximately {number(participation, 2)}% of bonded staking tokens, using the captured staking-pool denominator.</p>{source_list([(REFRESH + 'tally.json', 'Tally response (JSON)'), (REFRESH + 'votes.csv', str(refresh['ballot_count']) + ' ballots (CSV)'), (REFRESH + 'parameters.json', 'Approval thresholds (JSON)'), (REFRESH + 'capture_manifest.json', 'Capture log and source URLs'), ('/record/audit/native_4853_votes_all.csv', 'Earlier 26 October snapshot: 83 ballots')])}</section>
<section id="contracts" class="section-grid"><div class="section-intro"><h2>Three voting systems</h2></div><div class="reading"><h3>Native accounts and validators</h3><p>A delegator ballot overrides its validator’s option for that delegation. It does not erase the validator’s ballot or transfer ownership of the validator.</p><h3>The ampLUNA hub</h3><p>The hub holds native LUNA delegations. Its authorized vote operator can cast a normal or weighted native ballot. This affects native delegator power only—not Phoenix/TLA synthetic Alliance weight.</p><h3>The vAMP prop-gauge</h3><p>Vote-escrow holders accumulate proposal-specific voting power. After a 10% participation quorum, the gauge can ask the hub to cast a weighted or currently wanted vote. The large account can meet this quorum alone, but had not voted in the earlier 26 October gauge records. The refreshed gauge totals below do not identify individual voters.</p>{source_list([('https://github.com/erisprotocol/contracts-tokenfactory/blob/main/contracts/hub/src/gov.rs', 'Hub governance source'), ('https://github.com/erisprotocol/contracts-tokenfactory/blob/main/contracts/amp-governance/prop_gauges/src/vote.rs', 'Prop-gauge voting source'), ('https://github.com/erisprotocol/contracts-tokenfactory/blob/main/contracts/amp-governance/prop_gauges/src/queries.rs', 'Prop-gauge query source')])}</div></section>
<section id="scenario"><div class="section-heading"><h2>If the whole hub voted YES</h2><p>The calculation uses the {CAPTURE_LABEL} tally and approximately {number(Decimal(refresh['hub_total_uluna']) / Decimal(1000000000000), 4)}M LUNA delegated by the hub. It assumes no other changes.</p></div>{table(['Metric', 'Captured', 'Hypothetical full-hub YES'], [[label, millions(tally[key]), millions(refresh['full_hub_yes_scenario'][key])] for label, key in [('YES', 'yes_count'), ('NO', 'no_count'), ('NO WITH VETO', 'no_with_veto_count'), ('ABSTAIN', 'abstain_count')]] + [['YES / non-abstain', number(base_metrics['yes_share_non_abstain_percent'], 2) + '%', '<strong>' + number(scenario_metrics['yes_share_non_abstain_percent'], 2) + '%</strong>'], ['VETO / all votes', number(base_metrics['veto_share_all_percent'], 2) + '%', number(scenario_metrics['veto_share_all_percent'], 2) + '%'], ['YES majority', 'Below 50%', '<strong>Below 50%</strong>']])}<p class="note">The queries were made one after another; they are not confirmed to describe the same block. The first and last block responses are included with the downloads. The hub’s own ABSTAIN ballot overrides inherited validator options for its delegations. The calculation moves its bonded native delegation from ABSTAIN to YES; other ballots stay unchanged. It excludes synthetic Alliance weight. The result is approximate.</p><p class="context-note"><strong>The hub voted ABSTAIN.</strong> The native ballot is recorded among the {refresh['ballot_count']} ballots. The prop-gauge also records abstention voting power; its <code>current_vote</code> field is null, so that field alone cannot establish the native ballot. A weighted ballot could split the hub’s vote across options.</p>{table(['Validator operator', 'Validator ballot', 'Hub delegation', 'Effect of a hub YES vote'], fresh_validator_rows())}<p>The endpoint returns the same display name for every validator, so this table uses operator addresses. Full addresses and raw responses are included in the calculation download.</p>{source_list([(REFRESH + 'scenario.json', 'Calculation and assumptions (JSON)'), (REFRESH + 'hub_delegations.json', 'Hub delegations (JSON)'), (REFRESH + 'active_gauge.json', 'Gauge votes (JSON)'), (REFRESH + 'votes.json', 'Ballot response (JSON)'), ('/record/audit/governance_4853/scenario.json', 'Earlier 26 October scenario, retained for comparison')])}</section>
<section id="vamp" class="section-grid"><div class="section-intro"><h2>The vAMP quorum</h2></div><div><p>The 26 October records show total period-208 voting power of <strong>767,921 vAMP</strong>. The 10% quorum is approximately <strong>76,792</strong>. The 4853 gauge was active and weighted, with zero recorded votes in that 26 October capture. The refreshed capture above records abstention voting power.</p>{table(['Account', 'vAMP', 'Share'], [['Unattributed account <code>terra149h…mkk0z</code>', '215,228', '28.03%'], ['0xPhilipp ERIS account', '35,913', '4.68%'], ['ERIS protocol fee account', '47,338', '6.16%'], ['Arithmetic total of these three', '298,479', '38.87%']])}<p class="note">The unattributed account can meet the participation quorum alone. Combining three balances is a scenario only; it is not evidence of common ownership or a voting agreement.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/governance_4853/vamp_review.json', 'vAMP balances and sources'), ('/record/audit/governance_4853/vamp_review.json.source.md', 'Source queries and verification notes')])}</div></section>
<section id="lion-dao"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Lion DAO voted before its internal vote closed</h2></div><ol class="timeline"><li><time>24 Oct · 15:28 UTC</time><div><h3>RyanLion voted YES on A33</h3><p>The ballot carried 60.002B ROAR: 26.154% of total eligible power and 99.866% of YES power.</p></div></li><li><time>25 Oct · 05:06 UTC</time><div><h3>Lion DAO’s validator changed ABSTAIN to YES</h3><p>The DAO’s A33 vote was still open when its validator changed to YES.</p></div></li><li><time>26 Oct · 13:16 UTC</time><div><h3>A33’s voting window ended</h3><p>The final DAO ratio was 30.43% YES, 1.875% NO and 67.70% ABSTAIN. Both the DAO and validator ultimately favoured YES.</p></div></li></ol><p class="note"><strong>Attribution confidence:</strong> high for the transaction, ballot and voting-power facts; moderate for the DAO-interface display name “RyanLion,” pending a first-party profile declaration. No real-world identity is inferred.</p><p class="note">A Think Tank wallet transferred 513,657.706575846 ROAR to the RyanLion wallet on 22 October. The transfer and profile link support an operational relationship, not sole identity, ownership or motive. The transfer does not explain the much larger voting position.</p>{source_list([('https://chainsco.pe/terra2/tx/1D67ECAB44A5CC61825A210D051E1282A095A60B74DF614D4AD26CD38CDA556F', 'ROAR transfer transaction'), ('https://chainsco.pe/terra2/tx/4B356AF4C770CDD9729FDC39451C086BA188EF5A82EB7F1D0FCBE2AF28C5A0D2', 'Native validator YES transaction'), ('/record/audit/lion_dao/ryan_relationship.json', 'Download the relationship and A33 evidence')])}</section>
<section id="counterevidence" class="context-note"><h2>What remains unresolved</h2><p>Obtain the final tally and complete gauge history; entity-level interest disclosures; hosting and payment agreements; validator-mandate rules; historical Think Tank membership and notification records; and an explanation for the empty P5 veto message. Current evidence does not prove vote buying, a unified ERIS bloc, or ERIS control of the large vAMP account.</p></section>
{nextlinks(('/', 'Back: overview'), ('/stakeholders/', 'Next: who approves and benefits ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
page('4853 and voting power', voting, '/voting/')

# Stakeholders: every board seat, Think Tank member, ERIS signer and material validator group.
director_rows = []
for name, label in [('Philipp', '0xPhilipp'), ('MB_current', 'MB'), ('Vini', 'Vini'), ('Rose', 'Rose')]:
    p = profiles[name]
    c = dict(p['counts'])
    address = p['address']
    if name == 'MB_current':
        with (AUDIT / 'data/phoenix_board_votes_all.csv').open() as ballots:
            c = Counter(row['mb_combined_vote'] for row in csv.DictReader(ballots))
        address += ' (current); ' + profiles['MB_prior']['address'] + ' (prior)'
    director_rows.append([label, f"{c.get('yes', 0)} YES · {c.get('no', 0)} NO · {c.get('abstain', 0)} ABSTAIN · {c.get('not_recorded', 0)} no recorded ballot", address])
think_rows = [
    ['4719', '<code>terra13qjx…5zlz</code>', 'P1 YES; no later ballot', 'No specific economic attribution added'],
    ['7183', '<code>terra18gqp…muwjw</code>', 'P1 YES; no later ballot', '30,000-USDC Treasury grant'],
    ['David', '<code>terra1j00t…nsn0</code>', 'P1 YES; P3 ABSTAIN', 'Three scheduled 700-USDC delegation payments; separate 1,000 USDC'],
    ['Rebel', '<code>terra1rnwr…pmf</code>', 'No ballot on P1–P5', '7,500 USDC from ERIS fee account, memo “invoice”'],
    ['0xPhilipp', '<code>terra1vm5a…aywp</code>', 'Proposed P1–P5; ABSTAIN on all', 'Recipient/beneficiary overlaps; technical-message role'],
    ['RyanLion', '<code>terra1y835…l03e</code>', 'P1 YES; P2 ABSTAIN', 'ROAR transfer and Lion DAO relationship evidence'],
]
eris_votes = load('data/eris_multisig/votes.json')
eris_rows = []
labels = {
    'terra1alxk3fmhcl0ga80gl9yj9zp3ezjm4sdh8a9l9c': '0xPhilipp-associated',
    'terra18ruqkccl5tp493uhvra0u6jylrzq8t8dv5qs4c': 'Vini / board overlap',
}
for v in load('data/eris_multisig/voters.json')['data']['voters']:
    addr = v['addr']
    cast = [(pid, next(x['vote'] for x in votes if x['voter'] == addr)) for pid, votes in eris_votes.items() if any(x['voter'] == addr for x in votes)]
    voted = ', '.join(f'P{pid} {vote.upper()}' for pid, vote in cast) or 'No ballot'
    eris_rows.append([labels.get(addr, 'Address only; no handle asserted'), f"<code>{addr[:12]}…{addr[-6:]}</code>", str(v['weight']), voted])
stakeholders = head('Who approves and who benefits', 'The Phoenix board has four equal seats. Three YES votes approve an action. Some payment recipients also took part in approving those payments.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content">
<section class="reading" id="board-after-4853"><h2>What a removal would leave behind</h2><p>If Vini were removed, the ERIS/Creda and Superbolt relationships would remain among the other directors. Vini is a recorded dissenting voice, but Rose also opposed Estima and voted NO on A272. At an unchanged 75% threshold, three equal remaining seats would still need all three YES votes. A reduction to 60% would let any two approve.</p><a href="/voting/#remaining-board">4853’s possible effects and the safeguards needed</a></section><section id="board"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Phoenix board: four equal seats</h2><p>Each seat has 25 weight; 75% approval means three YES votes. The preserved history contains A1–A289.</p></div>{table(['Director', 'Preserved A1–A289 record', 'Board wallet'], [[r[0], r[1], f'<code>{r[2]}</code>'] for r in director_rows])}<div class="context-note"><strong>0xPhilipp’s roles.</strong> The established public handle/address links place 0xPhilipp in a Phoenix board seat, the Think Tank technical proposal role, the 50-of-90 ERIS multisig seat, the 4853 proposer/YES-voter role, an ERIS validator role and Treasury beneficiary/OTC relationships. These roles require consistent disclosure and independent checks; they do not prove one key, command structure or coordinated action.</div><div class="two-col"><article><h3>Directors voting on payments to themselves</h3><ul><li>A209 and A215: 0xPhilipp proposed and joined Vini and MB in enabling Creda’s 200,000-USDC first three milestones and 10,000-USDC analytics milestone.</li><li>A159: 0xPhilipp, Vini and Rose approved an action containing their own recurring compensation.</li><li>A249: Vini proposed and joined the minimum YES set for a 16,900-USDC audit payment to Vini’s address; Rose and MB also approved.</li><li>A271: 0xPhilipp, MB and Rose enabled Estima’s first 45,000-USDC payment; Vini was absent.</li></ul></article><article><h3>Proposals directors opposed</h3><ul><li>Rose supplied the only NO on A272; with Vini absent, it failed.</li><li>Vini and Rose rejected the earlier Estima A258; a reduced A266 later passed.</li><li>0xPhilipp and Rose rejected A288. MB’s only preserved NO was on the technically corrected A28.</li></ul></article></div>{source_list([('/record/audit/phoenix_board_votes_all.csv', 'Download the complete four-director voting matrix'), ('/record/audit/phoenix/director_vote_patterns.csv', 'Download pairwise and per-director vote patterns'), ('/record/audit/phoenix/key_proposals.json', 'Download key proposal responses')])}</section>
<section id="think-tank"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Think Tank: six voters, effectively 4-of-6</h2><p>A separate veto body—not merely an advisory group. Revoting is disabled and execution is members-only.</p></div>{table(['Member', 'Wallet', 'P1–P5 participation', 'Relevant disclosed/evidenced relationship'], think_rows)}<p>Across four substantive veto proposals, the five non-technical members supplied only two ballots out of 20 possible ballots, both abstentions. P5 contains no executable message; even four YES votes would not itself execute a Treasury veto.</p><p class="note">The technical member abstained on all five proposals. The records do not explain the other missing ballots, and membership has not been checked at every proposal’s block height.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/thinktank/votes.csv', 'Download all Think Tank ballots'), ('/record/audit/thinktank/proposals.json', 'Download all five proposals'), ('/record/audit/thinktank/members.json', 'Download current membership')])}</section>
<section id="eris-signers"><div class="section-heading"><h2>ERIS fixed multisig: five voters, 90 total weight</h2><p>The absolute threshold is 70. All recorded ballots in P1–P9 are YES; absences therefore matter when assessing how proposals passed or failed.</p></div>{table(['Attribution used', 'Wallet', 'Weight', 'P1–P9 ballots'], eris_rows)}<p>Proposal 9 passed with exactly 70 weight: the 50-weight signer plus two 10-weight signers. Lion DAO’s account executed it after passage; CW3 execution can be permissionless, so execution alone does not prove membership or coordination.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/eris_multisig_votes_all.csv', 'Download all ERIS multisig ballots'), ('/record/audit/eris_multisig/proposals.json', 'Download all nine proposals'), ('/record/audit/eris_multisig/transactions.json', 'Download preserved multisig transactions')])}</section>
<section id="validators"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Validators, contracts and large holders</h2></div><div class="two-col"><article><h3>Validator ballots differed</h3><p>Galactic Punks, ERIS and Orbital Command voted YES. Smart Stake and Ceres voted VETO. HighStakes voted ABSTAIN. Eleven reviewed allocation-linked validators had no current ballot.</p><p>Only BackBone Labs and cosmosrescue among those unvoted validators had material current hub delegations.</p></article><article><h3>The large vAMP account is unidentified</h3><p>The 215,228-vAMP account remains unattributed. It holds Galactic Punks NFTs and received several direct Galactic Punks transfers, but their reward or payment classification is unresolved.</p><p>NFT holdings, transfers, timing and protocol usage do not establish common ownership.</p></article></div><h3>Other paid contributors and counterparties</h3><p>Recurring core, comms, ambassador and moderator recipients; grant recipients; OTC counterparties; operations contracts; and the ERIS protocol fee account also need payment and approval records. Contract and module accounts must not be mislabeled as people.</p><a href="/treasury/">Continue to treasury and counterparty analysis {ARROW}</a></section>{nextlinks(('/voting/', 'Back: 4853 and voting'), ('/treasury/', 'Next: treasury controls ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
lock_facts = supplement['phoenix_A43_locks']['facts']
yes_wallets = {r['voter'] for r in lock_facts['ballots'] if r['vote'] == 'yes'}
lock_labels = {p['address']: name for name, p in profiles.items()}
lock_names = {'Philipp': '0xPhilipp', 'MB_prior': 'MB (prior board address)', 'Vini': 'Vini', 'Rose': 'Rose'}
lock_rows = [[lock_names.get(lock_labels.get(r['owner']), 'Public recipient address'), r['token_id'], number(r['amount_ampLUNA']),
              'YES · 25 weight' if r['owner'] in yes_wallets else 'No recorded ballot'] for r in lock_facts['locks']]
stakeholder_update = f'''<section id="director-review"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Vini and MB</h2></div><div class="two-col"><article><h3>Vini: audit beneficiary and overlapping signer</h3><p>Vini proposed A249’s 16,900-USDC audit payment to Vini’s address and supplied one of its three deciding YES votes. The alleged earlier recusal agreement remains unverified; audit scope and independent acceptance still need review.</p><p>Vini’s board address is also a 10-weight ERIS signer. Vini rejected the earlier Estima A258 and supported the later two-contract migration-admin transfer to the Phoenix board. October’s new Stewardship schedules omitted Vini while separate infrastructure payments continued; the reason is unresolved.</p></article><article><h3>MB: voting alignment and project relationships</h3><p>MB voted YES on 251 of 252 proposals authored by 0xPhilipp where MB cast a ballot. Many were routine operations; this is a reason to examine independent review, not proof of coordination.</p><p>The completed Treasury capture records 57,096 USDC to MB in its 2026 window. Two close-together claims represented separate entitlements. AADAO names MB as Superbolt’s representative; the full grant-to-distribution and delivery reconciliation remains open.</p></article></div><p class="note">Monitored Vini and MB wallet histories remain truncated. These records do not establish lifetime income or the absence of other relationships. Other directors approved Estima’s original A266 grant without 0xPhilipp; A272’s proposed removal and threshold reduction failed.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/phoenix/director_profiles.json', 'Director voting and scoped payment profiles'), ('/record/audit/phoenix/key_proposals.json', 'A249, Estima and A272 proposal records'), (FUNDING_REPORT, 'AADAO project funding and acceptance report')])}</section>
<section id="funded-locks"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Public funding also created voting locks</h2><p>On 5 November 2024, A43 created four permanent TLA locks funded with 83,000 ampLUNA in total.</p></div>{table(['Recipient role', 'Lock ID', 'ampLUNA funded', 'A43 ballot'], lock_rows, 'Original creation and ballots; later ownership and lock terms require separate history checks.')}<p>Three recipient wallets supplied the passing 75/100 YES weight. Phoenix A33 separately created four permanent locks with {number(supplement['phoenix_A33_locks']['facts']['total_ampLUNA'], 6)} ampLUNA in total. This is Phoenix A33, not Lion DAO A33.</p><p class="note">These are TLA voting locks, not native LUNA ballots. Current queries show fixed end periods for locks 429 and 432, with an additional 1 ampLUNA in 432. The change transactions and compliance with award terms remain unresolved; a changed state alone does not establish a breach.</p>{source_list([(LEADS + 'voting_locks.json', 'Funded locks with owners and event references'), (LEADS + 'queries/phoenix_A43_votes.json', 'A43 original ballot response'), (LEADS + 'verified_findings.json', 'Findings, current lock states and limitations')])}</section>
<section id="superbolt"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Superbolt: three beneficiary YES votes</h2></div><p>Neutron proposal 35 executed a 90,000-USDC batch on 5 August 2025: one 45,000-USDC payment and three 15,000-USDC payments. Its three recorded YES ballots came from three recipient wallets. This was collective approval, not a unilateral payment.</p><p>Separately, AADAO reports 80,000 dollars paid from an original 125,000-dollar Superbolt award, with 45,000 cancelled after rescoping. That upstream award has not been fully reconciled to the distribution. They are not additive funding, and neither amount is established as personal profit.</p>{source_list([(LEADS + 'queries/superbolt_35_proposal.json', 'Distribution proposal'), (LEADS + 'queries/superbolt_35_votes.json', 'All three recorded YES ballots'), (LEADS + 'verified_findings.json', 'Execution transfers and denomination check'), (FUNDING_REPORT, 'External award, rescoping and payment status')])}</section>'''
creda_approval = f'''<section id="creda-approval"><div class="section-heading"><h2>ERIS/Creda’s 210,000-USDC grant</h2><p>Treasury action 72 records a 210,000-USDC grant to the published ERIS address. All four milestones are marked claimed.</p></div>{table(['Approval', 'Milestones enabled', 'USDC', 'YES votes'], [['A209', 'Kickoff: 40,000; mainnet launch: 100,000; eMode + isolation: 60,000', '200,000', '0xPhilipp, Vini, MB'], ['A215', 'Analytics', '10,000', '0xPhilipp, Vini, MB']])}<p>0xPhilipp proposed both releases and supplied one of the three deciding YES votes on each. A director associated with the funded project took part in enabling its grant payments. The records show collective approval, not unilateral control.</p><p class="note">These proposals enabled milestones within an existing grant; they were not two new grants. Claimed status is recorded by the Treasury contract. Independent acceptance of every deliverable and personal profit are not established. Vini’s separate 16,900-USDC audit payment appears in the director review below.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/phoenix/key_proposals.json', 'A209 and A215: messages and all recorded ballots'), (LEADS + 'queries/review_treasury_action_72.json', 'Treasury action 72: recipient, amounts and claimed status'), ('/treasury/#project-funding', 'Other project grants and external support')])}</section>'''
stakeholders = stakeholders.replace('<section id="board"', creda_approval + '<section id="board"', 1)
stakeholders = insert_before_reading(stakeholders, stakeholder_update)
stakeholders = insert_before_reading(stakeholders, '<div class="context-note"><strong>About wallet labels.</strong> Public handles, roles and addresses are used only where the reviewed records support them. Address-level relationships do not establish exclusive custody, common ownership or intent.</div>')
page('Stakeholders and interests', stakeholders, '/stakeholders/')

# Treasury and forensic accounting.
treasury = head('Treasury and counterparties', 'Grants, compensation, asset trades and payments requiring further records.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><section id="funding-purpose"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Two different funding purposes</h2></div><div class="two-col"><article><h3>TLA and Treasury infrastructure</h3><p>Funding to build and operate tools for chain liquidity and community funds. The review asks whether the agreed governance safeguards and control handovers were delivered.</p><a href="/control/#approvals">Infrastructure terms and delivery</a></article><article><h3>Application development</h3><p>Creda, Votion and Estima received grants to build applications. Builder IP, grant payments and milestone acceptance are distinct from control of the chain’s Treasury.</p><a href="#project-funding">Application funding records</a></article></div></section><section class="section-grid"><div class="section-intro"><h2>What the payments represent</h2></div><div><p>The reviewed records mix grants, scheduled compensation, reimbursements, protocol operations, locked allocations and asset exchanges. Each needs different evidence. A large LUNA receipt may be an OTC trade; a contract receipt may be liquidity deployment; a scheduled claim may relate to an earlier entitlement period.</p><p>Do not add upstream grants and downstream distributions twice. Do not describe recurring transfers as vote buying without an agreement, communications or other direct evidence.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring validator-to-ERIS transfers</h2><p>Twenty transfers from February through October 2026; ten from each validator account. No memo establishes their purpose.</p></div>{table(['Sender', 'Transfers', 'Total', 'What is missing'], [['Galactic Punks', '10', '116,735 LUNA', 'Agreement, invoice, pricing formula and disclosure history'], ['Lion DAO', '10', '105,227 LUNA', 'Agreement, invoice, pricing formula and disclosure history'], ['<strong>Total</strong>', '<strong>20</strong>', '<strong>221,962 LUNA</strong>', 'Classification remains unresolved']])}<p>The purpose of these transfers is unresolved. Service terms are needed to classify them and establish whether the arrangement predates 4853.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/payment_summary.json', 'Download the payment summary and all 20 transfer locators'), ('/record/audit/transfers/monthly_validator_to_eris/', 'Open the preserved original transaction directory')])}</section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring payments beyond the board</h2><p>These recipients require the same invoice, entitlement-period and deliverable reconciliation as directors.</p></div>{table(['Recipient', 'Observed pattern', 'Review question'], [['<code>terra1azcp…j2tz2</code>', '65,000 USDC across 13 payments of 5,000', 'Core-work agreement and acceptance'], ['<code>terra12jrv…g7egn</code>', '32,500 USDC across 11 payments, primarily 3,000', 'Comms agreement and outputs'], ['<code>terra1ez76…clsxu</code>', '14,500 USDC across ten payments, primarily 1,500', 'Ambassador scope and outputs'], ['<code>terra1lqqs…meqs</code>', 'Recurring 1,800-USDC moderator schedules', 'Coverage period and approval'], ['<code>terra1xtyq…vy68</code>', 'Recurring 1,500-USDC moderator schedules', 'Coverage period and approval']])}</section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Director compensation and October schedules</h2></div><p>The 2026 coverage window records 57,096 USDC to MB, 66,480 USDC to Vini and 46,745.70 USDC to Rose under the report’s classifications. Vini’s total includes a 16,900-USDC audit payment and recurring infra payments. Rose separately received 237,323.047934 LUNA in an OTC trade supplying approximately 0.19657723 WBTC; that is not compensation.</p><p>October actions 118–120 created new Stewardship payments for 0xPhilipp, Rose and MB, but not Vini. Separate infra actions continued. The chain record does not explain the omission.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>OTC and related-party execution</h2></div><p>The 50-weight ERIS signer received 795,991.566014 LUNA through Treasury offers 91, 106 and 111. The checked messages are <code>execute_otc</code>, so these are asset exchanges, not salary. The governance questions are price fairness, approval, disclosure and execution quality.</p><p>Other large OTC recipients must likewise be valued using the asset they supplied and execution-time prices. LUNA totals alone do not establish expense, gain or loss.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Records needed to reconcile payments</h2></div><ol class="request-list"><li>Complete action exports: recipient, entitlement period, approval, claim, cancellation and transaction.</li><li>Contracts, invoices and pricing for monthly validator transfers and the ERIS invoice to Rebel.</li><li>Statements of work, competing quotes where relevant, measurable deliverables and independent acceptance.</li><li>Related-party OTC terms, supplied assets, market-rate checks and execution timestamps.</li><li>Beneficiary/decider overlap and recusal records for A159, A249, A266 and A271.</li><li>Admin and owner powers over recipients, schedules, fees, code and future voting.</li><li>Account continuity without assuming exclusive key custody; reconcile current and predecessor role wallets.</li><li>Direct wallet-flow graphs that distinguish native transfers, CW20 transfers, contract operations and module accounts.</li></ol></section>{nextlinks(('/stakeholders/', 'Back: stakeholders'), ('/governance/', 'Next: governance controls ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
funding_rows = [
    ['Creda', '210,000 USDC', 'Action 72; all four milestones marked claimed', 'No additional external grant verified here'],
    ['Votion', '50,000 USDC', 'Action 28; both milestones marked claimed; February 2025 award', '$50,000 for separate Hydro scope; funder reports payment and completion'],
    ['USDC migration app', '5,000 USDC', 'Action 109; completed funding action', 'No separate external development grant verified here'],
    ['Migration liquidity', '100,000 USDC', 'Action 110; liquidity capital, separate from developer compensation', 'No separate external contribution verified here'],
]
market = year_review['current_creda_metrics']
shares = year_review['approximate_share_supplied_percent']
funding_update = f'''<section id="project-funding"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Application grants and related liquidity support</h2><p>Creda, Votion and Estima are application-development grants. Grant funding alone does not establish transfer of the builder’s software IP. Their payment, conflict-of-interest and milestone-acceptance questions are separate from the <a href="/control/#approvals">TLA/Treasury control and handover review</a>. The table below retains the narrower project set checked against ERIS’s 2025–2026 retrospective.</p></div>{table(['Work', 'Phoenix Treasury support', 'Evidence and timing', 'External funding identified'], funding_rows, 'Identified project support, checked 27 October 2026; not a complete ERIS budget.')}<p>The identified direct project grants total <strong>{number(year_review['identified_named_project_grants_USDC'])} USDC</strong>. Votion’s earlier Phoenix award predates the article’s October 2025–October 2026 period; excluding it leaves <strong>{number(year_review['review_period_grants_excluding_earlier_votion_USDC'])} USDC</strong> in grants initiated within that period. The 100,000-USDC liquidity allocation is separate capital, not another development fee.</p><p><strong>Estima is a separate application award:</strong> A266 approved 125,000 USDC; A271 enabled the first 45,000 USDC, which the retained payment record shows paid. The remaining 80,000 USDC is not established as paid. Estima is outside the retrospective totals above. <a href="/control/#application-grants">Compare the application awards and their approval records.</a></p><p>Recurring Stewardship compensation is additional, but the record does not allocate it across each listed app or security task. Protocol fees and validator receipts also exist; their contribution to these development costs has not been reconciled.</p><p class="note">These amounts are project funding, not personal profit. The Hydro grant covers separate work, so the two awards do not establish duplicate billing. The records do not support a reliable Treasury-versus-external percentage for all the work in the article.</p>{source_list([(LEADS + 'year_review_analysis.json', 'Funding calculations and source records'), (LEADS + 'queries/review_treasury_action_72.json', 'Creda grant milestones'), (LEADS + 'queries/review_treasury_action_28.json', 'Earlier Votion grant'), (FUNDING_REPORT, 'AADAO: Votion paid and jointly verified complete'), (YEAR_ARTICLE, 'ERIS retrospective being assessed')])}</section>
<section id="creda-outcomes"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Creda deposits and borrowing</h2><p>Contract balances queried on 27 October are shown below. They do not establish performance throughout the year.</p></div>{table(['Metric', 'Captured value', 'Interpretation'], [['Supplied assets', '$' + number(market['total_supplied_usd']), 'Current contract-reported supply'], ['Borrowed assets', '$' + number(market['total_borrowed_usd']), 'Borrowing activity; not another pool of deposits'], ['Designated collateral', '$' + number(market['total_collateral_usd']), 'Overlaps supplied balances; do not add again'], ['Operations and board positions', number(shares['phoenix_operations_and_board'], 1) + '% of supply', 'Public Phoenix contract positions'], ['TLA single-pool position', number(shares['tla_single_pool'], 1) + '% of supply', 'Pooled user deposits; not established as entirely Treasury-funded']])}<p>The article’s current $1M TVL claim is not reproduced by the roughly $553,000 supply query or the market page. A different dated peak or TVL definition could explain it; the comparison does not establish that Creda never reached $1M.</p><p>Combined, the reviewed public-contract positions represent approximately 42% of supplied value. The remaining balance is not proven to be independent external demand. Queries were sequential, without one confirmed common block height, so the percentages are approximate.</p><p>At capture, the LUNA market had approximately 73.85% utilization and an 18.46% annual borrow rate. This shows borrowing at that time; it does not show demand throughout the year, the number of independent borrowers or whether development spending was justified.</p>{details('Delivery and security claims', '<p>The 10,000-USDC Creda analytics payment is verified, but payment is not independent milestone acceptance. A dated unfinished page would not establish that no analytics work existed. AADAO separately reports Votion’s Hydro milestones complete and jointly verified.</p><p>Zero historical bad debt and every borrower remaining healthy throughout the year were not established by the aggregate query. Public audit reports exist, but coverage must be matched to each deployed revision.</p><p>The article’s approximately $10M security attribution was not independently substantiated as a dated dollar calculation. Cosmos Labs documents collective recovery of already-stolen assets and losses on the Hub side; that does not disprove contributions elsewhere. Prevented theft, recovery custody and final restitution are different outcomes.</p>')}{source_list([(LEADS + 'year_review_analysis.json', 'Dated metrics, positions and arithmetic'), (LEADS + 'queries/review_creda_metrics.json', 'Exact metrics response'), ('https://creda.finance/markets', 'Creda market page; current state may differ'), ('https://forum.cosmos.network/t/neutron-governance-attack-cosmos-hub-response-and-recovery-update/17369', 'Cosmos Labs recovery account'), ('https://github.com/SCV-Security/PublicReports/tree/main/Eris%20Protocol', 'Published auditor reports')])}</section>'''
treasury = treasury.replace('<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring validator-to-ERIS transfers', funding_update + '<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring validator-to-ERIS transfers', 1)
treasury = insert_before_reading(treasury, '<section><h2>TLA and Treasury: funding to manage community assets</h2><p>The <a href="/control/">16 November control review</a> compares the terms for chain liquidity and Treasury infrastructure with delivered safeguards, retained authority and outstanding handovers. <a href="/control/#application-grants">Application-development grants</a> are a separate category; their funding does not itself require transfer of the builder’s IP or establish a TLA-style handover obligation.</p></section>')
page('Treasury and counterparties', treasury, '/treasury/')

